   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to requiring persons\noffering weight loss services to provide notice of certain risks",
    "basePrintNo": "S3764",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3764-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add Art 30-B §§641 - 645, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3764\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to requiring\npersons offering weight loss services to provide notice of certain risks\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nTo provide consumers with guidance and protection when choosing weight\nloss services and products.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nRequires anyone selling or offering to sell weight loss services or\nweight loss services and products as defined in the bill, in New York\nState to provide the following information in some written form:\n\n                  WEIGHT LOSS AND DIETING INFORMATION\n\n1. WARNING! Rapid weight loss may cause serious health problems. Rapid\nweight loss is weight loss of more than 1 1/2 to two lbs. per week or\nweight loss of more than one percent of body weight per week after the\nsecond week of participation in a weight loss program.\n\n2. Consult your physician before starting any weight loss program or\nusing any diet medications or formulas.\n\n3. Long term weight control is the safest and most important goal of any\ndiet program. Permanent lifestyle changes such as eating nutritious\nfoods and increasing physical activity help promote long term weight\nloss according to medical experts.\n\n4. Ask the person providing or selling you weight loss advice or diet\nproducts, medications or formulas about their qualifications and train-\ning in nutrition and health.\n\n5. You have the right to:\n\n(i) Ask questions about the potential health risks of this program or\nproduct, its nutritional content, and its psychological-support and\neducational components;\n\n(ii) Know the price of treatment, including the price of any extra\nproducts, services, supplements and laboratory tests; and\n\n(iii) Know the program duration of the program recommended to you.\n\nDefines weight loss services, products and direct sellers. Specifies how\nthe notice is to be provided. Exempts direct sellers, retail stores and\npharmacies that sell weight loss products unless they also provide\nservices or recommend the use of weight loss products. Exempts anyone\nproviding weight loss services incidental to their primary professional\nactivities.  Defines deceptive practices for sellers of weight loss\nservices and products. Grandfathers existing NYC notice, rules and\nallows for other local laws. Establishes civil penalties for violations.\n\n \nEFFECTS OF PRESENT LAW WHICH THIS BILL WOULD ALTER:\nAdds a new article 30-h to the General Business Law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMillions of New Yorkers have tried 'or are trying to lose weight for\nmedical and cosmetic reasons. It is estimated that dieters across the\nnation are spending as much as $33 billion each year on diet programs\nand products. Unfortunately the results of medical research about the\nsafety and effectiveness of diet programs are often vague and even\ncontradictory. This makes it difficult for consumers to decide what\nprograms and products to use. Success rates for most programs are very\nlow.\n\nMost experts now recommend long-term weight control by making permanent\nchanges in eating habits and increasing physical activity as opposed to\nquick fix diets. Many programs and products are offered or promoted by\ncelebrities, health \"experts\", store clerks and others who have no back-\nground or training in nutrition and health. Those offering the services\nsometimes advertise quick solutions that may be risky. The information\nand protection from deceptive practices required by this proposal help\nconsumers make a more informed choice before spending their money on\nweight loss. Legitimate programs and businesses should have no reason to\nbe concerned about providing this guidance to their customers. Diet\ncenters in NYC are already required to post similar information for\ntheir clients.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nPreviously introduced in Assembly.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become law.\n",
